Letting $x=-11$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{-2(-11+6)^n}{n\cdot(-5)^n}
 &= \displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{-2(-5)^n}{n\cdot(-5)^n}
 \\\\
&= -2\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{1}{n}
\end{align}$

This is a multiple of the harmonic series, which is known to diverge.

Letting $x=-1$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{-2(-1+6)^n}{n\cdot(-5)^n}
 &= \displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{-2(5)^n}{n\cdot(-5)^n}
 \\\\
 &= \displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{-2(-1)^n(-5)^n}{n\cdot(-5)^n}
 \\\\&= -2\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n}{n}
\end{align}$

This is a multiple of the alternating harmonic series, which is known to converge.
